  <section id="tradeoffs" class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <span class="sec-kicker">Architecture trade-offs</span>
        <h2>Why C++ arena parsers (<code>rapidyaml</code>) differ on structured workloads</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="callout blue">
        <h3>Safety, conformance, and ownership vs. raw mutable byte arenas</h3>
        <p>
          On geometric mean across the {HEADLINE.scenarioCount}-fixture suite, <code>ayaml</code> is
          faster than every measured Rust YAML library, C <code>libyaml</code>, C
          <code>libfyaml</code>, and C++ <code>fkYAML</code>. On dense multi-node documents, C++
          <code>rapidyaml</code> stays about
          <code>{HEADLINE.rymlAheadOfEvents.toFixed(2)}x</code> ahead of pull events and
          <code>{HEADLINE.rymlAheadOfValue.toFixed(2)}x</code> ahead of <code>ayaml value</code> on
          geometric mean because its output model does much less per-node work. It parses raw bytes
          into a flat, unsafe C++ index arena (optionally mutating the input buffer in place) with a
          handful of arena allocations (1 to 17 across fixtures), skipping Rust UTF-8 ownership
          checks, duplicate-key hashing, YAML 1.2.2 Core schema resolution on the arena path, and
          owned per-node <code>Value</code> / <code>Mapping</code> structures.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>ayaml</code> enforces <code>#![forbid(unsafe_code)]</code>, YAML 1.2.2 Core schema
          semantics, duplicate mapping-key rejection, byte-accurate spans, bounded alias-expansion
          budgets, and full Serde integration. On bulk plain text (<code>large-scalar-1mb</code>, a
          1,000,000-byte single-line plain scalar), <code>ayaml::parse_events</code> runs at
          <strong>{HEADLINE.largeScalarMib.toFixed(1)} MiB/s</strong>, compared to
          <strong>{HEADLINE.largeScalarLibfyamlMib.toFixed(1)} MiB/s</strong> for C
          <code>libfyaml</code> events and
          <strong>{HEADLINE.largeScalarVsRyml.toFixed(2)}x faster than rapidyaml</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="reproduce" class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <span class="sec-kicker">Environment, peer versions &amp; methodology</span>
        <h2>Hardware, compiler flags, peer versions, and caveats</h2>
        <p class="sec-lede">
          Pure-Rust <code>ayaml</code> benchmarks and the deterministic <code>ayaml-fixtures</code>
          catalog live in <code>asamarts/ayaml</code> (run via <code>cargo xtask</code>). Full
          11-parser Rust/C/C++ comparisons run from the standalone public harness
          <a href={HARNESS_REPO} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>asamarts/ayaml-benchmarks</code></a>
          so the core <code>ayaml</code> repository stays 100% pure Rust with zero C/C++ build dependencies.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prov-grid">
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table" aria-label="Benchmark hardware, compiler, and provenance metadata">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">CPU &amp; microarchitecture</th>
                <td>
                  {fingerprint.cpu}
                  {fingerprint.cpu_microarch && (
                    <>
                      <br />
                      <span>Microarchitecture: {fingerprint.cpu_microarch}</span>
                    </>
                  )}
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">OS &amp; kernel</th>
                <td>
                  {fingerprint.os}/{fingerprint.arch}
                  {fingerprint.kernel ? ` (${fingerprint.kernel})` : ''}, {fingerprint.cpu_count} logical cores
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Isolation</th>
                <td>
                  Pinned CPU core <code>{fingerprint.cpu_pin}</code>,
                  <code>{fingerprint.governor}</code> frequency governor
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Rust compiler</th><td><code>{fingerprint.rustc}</code></td></tr>
              {fingerprint.c_compiler && (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">C / C++ compilers</th>
                  <td>
                    <code>{fingerprint.c_compiler}</code> / <code>{fingerprint.cxx_compiler}</code>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              )}
              {fingerprint.build_flags && (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Build &amp; LTO flags</th>
                  <td>
                    Rust: <code>{fingerprint.build_flags.rust_profile}</code><br />
                    C: <code>{fingerprint.build_flags.c_flags}</code><br />
                    C++: <code>{fingerprint.build_flags.cxx_flags}</code>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              )}
              {fingerprint.criterion && (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Criterion settings</th>
                  <td>
                    <code>warm_up={fingerprint.criterion.warm_up_ms}ms</code>,
                    <code>measurement={fingerprint.criterion.measurement_ms}ms</code>,
                    <code>sample_size={fingerprint.criterion.sample_size}</code>,
                    <code>rounds={fingerprint.criterion.rounds}</code> (median of round medians)
                  </td>
                </tr>
              )}
              {fingerprint.variance && (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Cross-round variance (CV)</th>
                  <td>
                    Median <code>{fingerprint.variance.median_round_cv_pct.toFixed(3)}%</code>,
                    95th percentile <code>{fingerprint.variance.percentile_95_round_cv_pct.toFixed(3)}%</code>,
                    max <code>{fingerprint.variance.max_round_cv_pct.toFixed(3)}%</code> across {fingerprint.variance.rounds} rounds
                  </td>
                </tr>
              )}
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Comparative harness</th>
                <td>
                  <a href={HARNESS_SHA ? `${HARNESS_REPO}/commit/${HARNESS_SHA}` : HARNESS_REPO} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    <code>asamarts/ayaml-benchmarks@{HARNESS_SHA ? HARNESS_SHA.slice(0, 12) : 'main'}</code>
                  </a>
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Measured ayaml commit</th><td><code>{fingerprint.public_git_sha}</code></td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">v0.2.3 engine baseline</th><td><code>{fingerprint.baseline_v023_git_sha}</code> (first commit carrying <code>ayaml-fixtures</code>, identical engine to tag <code>v0.2.3</code>)</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Fixture manifest SHA-256</th><td><code>{fingerprint.fixture_manifest_sha256}</code></td></tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Detailed methodology</th>
                <td><a href={`${BASE}/docs/benchmarks/`}>Benchmark methodology guide</a></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="prov-side">
          {PEER_VERSION_ENTRIES.length > 0 && (
            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table class="data-table" aria-label="Pinned peer crate and C/C++ library versions">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Library / crate</th>
                    <th scope="col">Pinned version</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {PEER_VERSION_ENTRIES.map(([name, ver]) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td><code>{name}</code></td>
                      <td><code>{ver}</code></td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          )}

          <div class="code-frame" style="margin-top: var(--s4);">
            <div class="code-bar">
              <span>Reproduce locally</span>
              <button class="copy-btn" type="button" data-copy={REPRO_COMMANDS}>Copy commands</button>
            </div>
            <Code
              code={REPRO_COMMANDS}
              lang="bash"
              themes={{ light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' }}
              defaultColor={false}
            />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout" style="margin-top: var(--s5);">
        <h3>Disclosed methodology caveats &amp; semantic differences</h3>
        <ul class="caveat-list">
          <li>
            <strong><code>serde-saphyr</code> DOM target:</strong> <code>serde-saphyr</code> does
            not expose a YAML-native <code>Value</code> DOM, so Tier 2 deserializes into
            <code>serde_json::Value</code> (string-only object keys, <code>BTreeMap</code>-backed
            ordered keys, no YAML tags).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Per-peer scenario exclusions:</strong> Every parser is checked by a pre-timing
            semantic oracle and excluded only where its API or upstream limits cannot parse a
            fixture: <code>ayaml serde-value</code>, <code>ayaml compat-serde</code>,
            <code>fkYAML</code>, and <code>yaml-serde</code> use single-document APIs and exclude
            multi-document <code>cluster-manifests</code>; <code>noyalib</code> excludes
            <code>aliases-1000</code> and <code>container-compose</code>; <code>serde-saphyr</code>
            excludes <code>aliases-1000</code>; <code>libfyaml events</code> covers all 26 scenarios
            using raw non-resolving <code>FYPCF_QUIET</code> events (no alias expansion, matching
            <code>ayaml::parse_events</code> and <code>libyaml events</code>); and
            <code>libfyaml doc</code> deterministically excludes <code>cluster-manifests</code>,
            <code>depth-64</code> (upstream max depth 64), <code>short-map-20000</code>,
            <code>short-explicit-20000</code>, <code>scale-map-100kb</code>,
            <code>scale-map-1mb</code>, and <code>scale-openapi-1mb</code> (quadratic flat-map
            construction) across both timing and memory.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Duplicate-key &amp; insertion-order semantics:</strong> <code>ayaml</code>
            enforces duplicate mapping-key rejection and preserves insertion order via
            <code>indexmap</code>. <code>rapidyaml</code> and <code>libyaml</code> skip duplicate
            hashing during parsing; <code>fkYAML</code> stores mappings in sorted
            <code>std::map</code>; <code>serde_json::Value</code> uses <code>BTreeMap</code>;
            <code>yaml-rust2</code> and <code>saphyr</code> use <code>LinkedHashMap</code> /
            <code>Hash</code> with differing duplicate policies.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong><code>libfyaml</code> portable build &amp; <code>ayaml scan</code>:</strong>
            <code>libfyaml</code> is compiled with <code>HAVE_PORTABLE_TARGET=1</code> (portable C
            without host-specific SIMD flags, matching default Rust <code>target-cpu</code>).
            <code>ayaml scan</code> uses the <code>bench-internals</code> feature to isolate the
            tokenizer; <code>ayaml events</code> uses the public pull-based
            <code>ayaml::parse_events</code> iterator.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Synthetic deterministic fixtures:</strong> The <code>ayaml-fixtures</code>
            catalog uses deterministic seeded generators representing common YAML shapes (CI
            workflows, OpenAPI specs, multi-doc manifests, lockfiles, scaling ladders, and stress
            cases) rather than raw third-party repositories. Treat published numbers as reproducible
            baselines and always benchmark on your own production documents.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

